drm: h713: mirror the panel in hardware (installation mode, plane reflect)

A projector mounted on the ceiling or behind a rear-projection screen
needs its whole picture mirrored. The stock firmware does it in display
hardware (Installation mode -> PANEL_CONFIG_MIRROR -> the MIPS window
layer); with the MIPS parked, this driver now does the same.

The recipe is from static RE of display.bin, self-tested against stock
captures of all four modes (docs/reference/mirror-recipe-2026-10-06.md),
and checked on the panel and with the panel's own histogram
(docs/reference/mirror-phase1-2026-10-06.md):

 - H is the panel's line reversal, lvds+0x0a0[0]. It mirrors whatever the
   selector feeds the panel, position and bars included, and costs one
   line. The video route compensates upstream, as the firmware does: vde
   - 1 on the dy-timed NR/proc fields and the border overlay's y. The RGB
   route has no upstream to move, so its program window (lvds+0x0c0)
   starts one line later instead. Without either, the histogram shows one
   edge row lost; with them it is bit-identical to the upright picture.
 - V is a bottom-up fetch: source 0 CTRL[3:2] = 2 with comp+0x040[3]
   cleared inside NR's hold for video, OSD CTRL bit 3 for the RGB route.
   It moves nothing; the histogram is bit-identical. OSD bit 2 is dead.

Two inputs, combined by XOR:
 - the installation mode, which mirrors the whole screen and is invisible
   to clients: the sysfs attribute mirror_mode (0 front, 1 rear H,
   2 rear ceiling V, 3 ceiling H+V) re-commits every plane at once,
   because a warm reboot costs this display; the mirror_mode module
   parameter sets it at probe;
 - the DRM rotation property on both planes (REFLECT_X, REFLECT_Y,
   ROTATE_180), which mirrors one plane's picture inside its rectangle.
Placement is mapped to suit: x is pre-mirrored for a plane REFLECT_X,
which the panel would move, and y for the installation V, which the
fetch would not. The panel bit and window are shared, so whichever route
owns the exclusive mux writes them, and video_stop hands them back to the
RGB route's values.

It needs the letterbox window layer and a board that booted unmirrored in
AFBD source mode 2, which is the base the recipe is relative to;
otherwise it reports itself unavailable and creates no rotation property.
debugfs h713_geometry prints the committed picture, its flips, the mirror
registers, and every window-layer register against the model.

Also: the first-frame cover (0165) now touches only the border words. The
new overlay-rectangle words sit in the same block, and a blanket cover
would have overwritten them.

